Show native views on Windows: the app's composition content under GPUI's frame, through DirectComposition.

What: `WindowsWindow::attach_native_view` takes any COM object `IDCompositionVisual::SetContent` takes (usually a
composition swap chain the app presents to on its own device and thread) and returns an attachment whose drop takes
it out again; `native_view_input` is 0049's `Window`, as a DirectComposition visual takes no input. The renderer's
DirectComposition tree becomes, back to front under one root visual: a background visual, a container of one
subtree per native view (a clip visual holding a content visual, after Chromium's `VisualSubtree`), and GPUI's own
visual, which shows its swap chain as upstream (0058 keeps it in step with the views). Each frame,
`NativeViewTree::place_views` (named after macOS's `Layout::places_views`) compares the scene's views
(`native_views.rs`: placement from core's `native_view_placement::visible`, back-to-front order, background colour from
`native_view_placement::background`, black when none asks) with what the tree
shows, restacks only when the views or their order change, places only the views that moved, and the frame commits
once after (Chromium's `DCLayerTree::CommitAndClearPendingOverlays` order: present, then build and commit the tree).
`NativeView::set_background`'s colour
(black by default, as on macOS) fills a 1×1 swap chain stretched under the views, shown only for an opaque window
while a view is shown, where a view's content hasn't caught up with its element. A lost device rebuilds every view's
visuals from the content the app gave. A view whose last handle is dropped while the renderer is borrowed (it draws)
is taken out of the tree when the next frame starts, never left behind. The code is in `native_views.rs` (pure
decisions, new) and `directx_renderer/native_view_tree.rs` (the visual tree and the attachment, next to 0056's hole
pipeline); `directx_renderer.rs`, `window.rs` and `gpui_windows.rs` get only hooks (fields, a call per stage, the
module rows).

Why: GPUI on Windows already presents through DirectComposition, so the
app's content is one more visual in the same tree: no child window (no airspace problem: GPUI's window keeps all
input), no copy of the app's frames, and the app's presents reach the screen without GPUI drawing or committing
(DWM picks up a composition swap chain's present by itself).
Modelled on Chromium's ui/gl/dc_layer_tree.cc @
4fc26f32cd03f0e8443c4aa11d6859a901d90659 (BSD-3-Clause, third_party/chromium/PORTED.md). Over 200 lines: the tree
(attach, place, restack, recover after a lost device) is one concern that splits no further, and it is modelled on
Chromium rather than a near-verbatim port, so patch rule 4's port exemption does not cover it; flagged for the owner.

Test: `native_views.rs`'s tables (placement per layout, frame stacking and background) run on any host through
`scripts/patch-test.sh` (windows-pure). Live: `scripts/windows-native-view-checks.sh` on CI's Windows runner reads
the composed screen back at 100 and 125 % (docs/native-view-windows.md); this patch's checks: input over the view
reaches its element with positions in the view; 0 GPUI frames per second while only the view presents.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

